@avlp/pipeline-worker:dev: {"timestamp":"2026-10-07T21:43:15.232Z","level":"info","event":"provider.together.response","fields":{"provider":"together","endpoint":"/chat/completions","response":{"httpStatus":200,"contentType":"text/event-stream","providerRequestId":"42e52b17-aws_ew1","model":"moonshotai/Kimi-K3","text":"{\n  \"schemaVersion\": \"objectives-v2\",\n  \"objectives\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Explain rest as an active physiological process by describing the five mechanisms (memory consolidation, cardiovascular and metabolic recalibration, immune regulation, tissue repair, and stress-axis downregulation) that occur during adequate sleep and recovery.\",\n      \"measurable_verb\": \"explain\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"],\n      \"confidence\": 0.95\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Analyze the neurocognitive and affective costs of sleep loss by identifying specific deficits in attention, working memory, decision-making, and emotional control, and evaluating the strength of evidence from meta-analytic research spanning 150+ studies.\",\n      \"measurable_verb\": \"analyze\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\"],\n      \"confidence\": 0.95\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Differentiate the temporal trajectory of cardiovascular risk by explaining how sympathetic activation, inflammation, blood-pressure dysregulation, and hormonal stress responses link chronic short sleep to hypertension, coronary disease, and stroke—emphasizing that risk accrues over months and years, not from isolated poor nights.\",\n      \"measurable_verb\": \"differentiate\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"],\n      \"confidence\": 0.9\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Trace the metabolic progression from sleep restriction to type 2 diabetes risk by connecting reduced insulin sensitivity, increased hunger and caloric intake, and the trajectory toward insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome.\",\n      \"measurable_verb\": \"trace\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"],\n      \"confidence\": 0.9\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Evaluate the bidirectional relationship between sleep and mental health by analyzing how disrupted sleep worsens emotional regulation, anxiety, depressive symptoms, and stress tolerance while psychiatric states simu","textChunks":["{\n  \"schemaVersion\": \"objectives-v2\",\n  \"objectives\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Explain rest as an active physiological process by describing the five mechanisms (memory consolidation, cardiovascular and metabolic recalibration, immune regulation, tissue repair, and stress-axis downregulation) that occur during adequate sleep and recovery.\",\n      \"measurable_verb\": \"explain\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"],\n      \"confidence\": 0.95\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Analyze the neurocognitive and affective costs of sleep loss by identifying specific deficits in attention, working memory, decision-making, and emotional control, and evaluating the strength of evidence from meta-analytic research spanning 150+ studies.\",\n      \"measurable_verb\": \"analyze\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\"],\n      \"confidence\": 0.95\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Differentiate the temporal trajectory of cardiovascular risk by explaining how sympathetic activation, inflammation, blood-pressure dysregulation, and hormonal stress responses link chronic short sleep to hypertension, coronary disease, and stroke—emphasizing that risk accrues over months and years, not from isolated poor nights.\",\n      \"measurable_verb\": \"differentiate\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"],\n      \"confidence\": 0.9\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Trace the metabolic progression from sleep restriction to type 2 diabetes risk by connecting reduced insulin sensitivity, increased hunger and caloric intake, and the trajectory toward insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome.\",\n      \"measurable_verb\": \"trace\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"],\n      \"confidence\": 0.9\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Evaluate the bidirectional relationship between sleep and mental health by analyzing how disrupted sleep worsens emotional regulation, anxiety, depressive symptoms, and stress tolerance while psychiatric states simu","ltaneously disturb sleep architecture.\",\n      \"measurable_verb\": \"evaluate\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"6b86335a-0ad4-72b2-bb3f-d7433f3a0ea3\"],\n      \"confidence\": 0.9\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Apply the stress → recovery → adaptation model to distinguish between acute fatigue and chronic underrecovery, and justify why active recovery strategies often outperform complete inactivity for restoring function across physiological systems.\",\n      \"measurable_verb\": \"apply\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\", \"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"],\n      \"confidence\": 0.88\n    }\n  ],\n  \"keyConcepts\": [\n    {\n      \"concept\": \"Rest as active physiological process\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"],\n      \"description\": \"Rest involves active biological maintenance: memory consolidation, cardiovascular and metabolic recalibration, immune regulation, tissue repair, and nervous system downregulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"concept\": \"Neurocognitive and affective costs of sleep loss\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\"],\n      \"description\": \"Sleep loss consistently impairs attention, reaction time, working memory, learning, decision-making, and emotional control, with meta-analytic evidence showing reduced positive mood and increased anxiety.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"concept\": \"Cardiovascular strain mechanisms\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"],\n      \"description\": \"Chronic short sleep affects sympathetic nervous system activity, inflammation, blood-pressure regulation, and hormonal stress responses, leading to hypertension, coronary heart disease, and stroke over months and years.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"concept\": \"Metabolic dysregulation\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"],\n      \"description\": \"Sleep restriction reduces insulin sensitivity and increases hunger and calorie intake, contributing to weight gain, insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome, and type 2"," diabetes risk.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"concept\": \"Immune-system dysregulation\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"cda37927-8591-7c52-bb5e-8f3499aa046e\"],\n      \"description\": \"Sleep deprivation disrupts immune signalling and increases inflammatory activity, with controlled studies showing elevated IL-6 and C-reactive protein after partial sleep deprivation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"concept\": \"Bidirectional sleep-mental health relationship\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"6b86335a-0ad4-72b2-bb3f-d7433f3a0ea3\"],\n      \"description\": \"Sleep and mental health influence each other: anxiety and depression disturb sleep, while insufficient sleep worsens emotional regulation, stress tolerance, and positive emotion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"concept\": \"Recovery debt in training\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"f823c9f8-9fd0-7db3-931a-574c5002b2b1\"],\n      \"description\": \"When training stress repeatedly exceeds recovery capacity, individuals progress from functional fatigue to non-functional overreaching and potentially overtraining syndrome.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"concept\": \"Stress → recovery → adaptation cycle\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\"],\n      \"description\": \"The body operates on a stress-recovery-adaptation cycle; chronic underrecovery (not occasional poor sleep) drives long-term physiological strain and disease risk.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"concept\": \"Active recovery vs. complete inactivity\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"],\n      \"description\": \"Active recovery (walking, moderate exercise, hobbies, socializing) can be more restorative than complete inactivity, depending on the type of fatigue being addressed.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"prerequisiteKnowledge\": [\n    {\n      \"prerequisite\": \"Basic understanding of autonomic nervous system function (sympathetic vs. parasympathetic activation)\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"prerequisite\": \"Familiarity with metabolic terminology (insulin sensitivity, metabolic syndrome)\",\n      \"","blockIds\": [\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"prerequisite\": \"Knowledge of inflammatory markers and immune signalling\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"cda37927-8591-7c52-bb5e-8f3499aa046e\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"prerequisite\": \"Understanding of exercise physiology concepts (training stress, adaptation)\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"f823c9f8-9fd0-7db3-931a-574c5002b2b1\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"prerequisite\": \"Awareness of cognitive domains (attention, working memory, executive function)\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"vocabulary\": [\n    {\n      \"term\": \"Memory consolidation\",\n      \"definition\": \"The process by which the brain stabilizes and integrates newly acquired information during sleep\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"term\": \"Sympathetic activation\",\n      \"definition\": \"Increased activity of the sympathetic nervous system, associated with stress responses and cardiovascular strain\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"term\": \"Insulin sensitivity\",\n      \"definition\": \"The degree to which cells respond to insulin; reduced sensitivity impairs blood-sugar regulation\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"term\": \"IL-6 (Interleukin-6)\",\n      \"definition\": \"An inflammatory marker that increases after partial sleep deprivation\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"cda37927-8591-7c52-bb5e-8f3499aa046e\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"term\": \"C-reactive protein\",\n      \"definition\": \"An inflammatory marker elevated following sleep deprivation, indicating increased inflammatory activity\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"cda37927-8591-7c52-bb5e-8f3499aa046e\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"term\": \"Non-functional overreaching\",\n      \"definition\": \"A state where training stress exceeds recovery capacity, leading to persistent fatigue and declining performance\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"f823c9f8-9fd0-7db3-931a-574c5002b2b1\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"term\":"," \"Overtraining syndrome\",\n      \"definition\": \"Severe condition resulting from chronic training-recovery imbalance, characterized by persistent fatigue, performance decline, and mood disturbance\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"f823c9f8-9fd0-7db3-931a-574c5002b2b1\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"term\": \"Chronic underrecovery\",\n      \"definition\": \"Consistently inadequate recovery over extended periods, including insufficient sleep, lack of breaks, or prolonged psychological stress\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"term\": \"Active recovery\",\n      \"definition\": \"Low-intensity activities (walking, stretching, hobbies) that support physiological and psychological restoration\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"likelyMisconceptions\": [\n    {\n      \"misconception\": \"One poor night of sleep causes heart disease\",\n      \"correction\": \"Cardiovascular risk accrues over months and years of chronic short sleep, not from isolated poor nights. The concern is repeated inadequate recovery.\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"misconception\": \"Sleeping five hours instead of eight gains three productive hours\",\n      \"correction\": \"While clock hours increase, the quality of waking hours declines. Attention, creativity, memory, judgement, and reaction time deteriorate, so fewer well-rested hours can outperform longer exhausted periods.\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"e312964f-0a23-7c7e-8697-7c3ad685eebe\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"misconception\": \"Rest means complete inactivity or lying in bed\",\n      \"correction\": \"Active recovery (walking, moderate exercise, hobbies) can be more beneficial than complete inactivity. Someone mentally exhausted may benefit more from a walk than from lying in bed scrolling social media.\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"misconception\": \"More sleep is always healthier\",\n      \"correction\": \"Excessive time in bed is n","ot necessarily healthier. Very long habitual sleep is associated with poorer health outcomes, though long sleep may be a marker of underlying illness rather than the cause.\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"misconception\": \"Occasional poor sleep or a busy week causes major permanent harm\",\n      \"correction\": \"An occasional poor night or busy week is unlikely to cause major permanent harm in healthy individuals. The clinical concern is chronic underrecovery sustained over months.\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"assessmentQuestions\": [\n    {\n      \"question\": \"Describe the five active physiological processes that occur during adequate sleep and recovery, and explain why rest should not be considered 'doing nothing.'\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"question\": \"A patient reports sleeping 5 hours nightly to 'gain productive time.' Using evidence from the source, explain why this strategy may be counterproductive for knowledge work.\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\", \"e312964f-0a23-7c7e-8697-7c3ad685eebe\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"question\": \"Explain the mechanism linking chronic short sleep to cardiovascular disease, and clarify why a single poor night does not cause heart disease.\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"question\": \"Trace the metabolic pathway from sleep restriction to increased type 2 diabetes risk, including changes in insulin sensitivity and energy intake.\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"question\": \"Compare the immune consequences of partial sleep deprivation with the immune benefits of normal sleep, citing specific inflammatory markers.\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"cda37927-8591-7c52-bb5e-8f3499aa046e\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"question\": \"An athlete presents with persistent fatigue, declining performance, and mood ","disturbance after months of intense training. Using the stress → recovery → adaptation model, explain the likely diagnosis and the importance of recovery.\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"f823c9f8-9fd0-7db3-931a-574c5002b2b1\", \"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"question\": \"A client asks whether they should rest completely in bed or take a walk after a mentally exhausting workday. Provide an evidence-based recommendation.\",\n      \"blockIds\": [\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"focusCoverage\": {\n    \"status\": \"covered\"\n  }\n}"],"textChunksTruncated":false,"textCharacters":12561,"reasoningCharacters":0,"finishReason":"stop","inputTokens":"[REDACTED]","outputTokens":"[REDACTED]"},"retries":0,"latencyMs":46064,"service":"pipeline-worker","correlationId":"01a11850-8e35-73cc-a9b5-000a01137296"}}
