@avlp/pipeline-worker:dev: {"timestamp":"2026-10-07T21:43:50.616Z","level":"info","event":"provider.together.response","fields":{"provider":"together","endpoint":"/chat/completions","response":{"httpStatus":200,"contentType":"text/event-stream","providerRequestId":"7d4f0b67-aws_ew1","model":"moonshotai/Kimi-K3","text":"{\"schemaVersion\":\"objectives-v2\",\"objectives\":[{\"statement\":\"Explain rest as an active physiological process by describing the five mechanisms (memory consolidation, cardiovascular and metabolic recalibration, immune regulation, tissue repair, and stress-axis downregulation) that occur during adequate sleep and recovery.\",\"verb\":\"explain\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"],\"confidence\":0.95},{\"statement\":\"Analyze the neurocognitive and affective costs of sleep loss by identifying specific deficits in attention, working memory, decision-making, and emotional control, and evaluating the strength of evidence from meta-analytic research spanning 150+ studies.\",\"verb\":\"analyze\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\"],\"confidence\":0.95},{\"statement\":\"Differentiate the temporal trajectory of cardiovascular risk by explaining how sympathetic activation, inflammation, blood-pressure dysregulation, and hormonal stress responses link chronic short sleep to hypertension, coronary disease, and stroke—emphasizing that risk accrues over months and years, not from isolated poor nights.\",\"verb\":\"differentiate\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"],\"confidence\":0.9},{\"statement\":\"Trace the metabolic progression from sleep restriction to type 2 diabetes risk by connecting reduced insulin sensitivity, increased hunger and caloric intake, and the trajectory toward insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome.\",\"verb\":\"trace\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"],\"confidence\":0.9},{\"statement\":\"Evaluate the bidirectional relationship between sleep and mental health by analyzing how disrupted sleep worsens emotional regulation, anxiety, depressive symptoms, and stress tolerance while psychiatric states simultaneously disturb sleep architecture.\",\"verb\":\"evaluate\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"6b86335a-0ad4-72b2-bb3f-d7433f3a0ea3\"],\"confidence\":0.9},{\"statement\":\"Apply the stress → recovery → adaptation model to distinguish","textChunks":["{\"schemaVersion\":\"objectives-v2\",\"objectives\":[{\"statement\":\"Explain rest as an active physiological process by describing the five mechanisms (memory consolidation, cardiovascular and metabolic recalibration, immune regulation, tissue repair, and stress-axis downregulation) that occur during adequate sleep and recovery.\",\"verb\":\"explain\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"],\"confidence\":0.95},{\"statement\":\"Analyze the neurocognitive and affective costs of sleep loss by identifying specific deficits in attention, working memory, decision-making, and emotional control, and evaluating the strength of evidence from meta-analytic research spanning 150+ studies.\",\"verb\":\"analyze\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\"],\"confidence\":0.95},{\"statement\":\"Differentiate the temporal trajectory of cardiovascular risk by explaining how sympathetic activation, inflammation, blood-pressure dysregulation, and hormonal stress responses link chronic short sleep to hypertension, coronary disease, and stroke—emphasizing that risk accrues over months and years, not from isolated poor nights.\",\"verb\":\"differentiate\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"],\"confidence\":0.9},{\"statement\":\"Trace the metabolic progression from sleep restriction to type 2 diabetes risk by connecting reduced insulin sensitivity, increased hunger and caloric intake, and the trajectory toward insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome.\",\"verb\":\"trace\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"],\"confidence\":0.9},{\"statement\":\"Evaluate the bidirectional relationship between sleep and mental health by analyzing how disrupted sleep worsens emotional regulation, anxiety, depressive symptoms, and stress tolerance while psychiatric states simultaneously disturb sleep architecture.\",\"verb\":\"evaluate\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"6b86335a-0ad4-72b2-bb3f-d7433f3a0ea3\"],\"confidence\":0.9},{\"statement\":\"Apply the stress → recovery → adaptation model to distinguish"," between acute fatigue and chronic underrecovery, and justify why active recovery strategies often outperform complete inactivity for restoring function across physiological systems.\",\"verb\":\"apply\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\",\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"],\"confidence\":0.88}],\"keyConcepts\":[{\"text\":\"Rest as active physiological process: Rest involves active biological maintenance including memory consolidation, cardiovascular and metabolic recalibration, immune regulation, tissue repair, and nervous system downregulation.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"]},{\"text\":\"Neurocognitive and affective costs of sleep loss: Sleep loss consistently impairs attention, reaction time, working memory, learning, decision-making, and emotional control, with meta-analytic evidence showing reduced positive mood and increased anxiety.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\"]},{\"text\":\"Cardiovascular strain mechanisms: Chronic short sleep affects sympathetic nervous system activity, inflammation, blood-pressure regulation, and hormonal stress responses, leading to hypertension, coronary heart disease, and stroke over months and years.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"]},{\"text\":\"Metabolic dysregulation: Sleep restriction reduces insulin sensitivity and increases hunger and calorie intake, contributing to weight gain, insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome, and type 2 diabetes risk.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"]},{\"text\":\"Immune-system dysregulation: Sleep deprivation disrupts immune signalling and increases inflammatory activity, with controlled studies showing elevated IL-6 and C-reactive protein after partial sleep deprivation.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"cda37927-8591-7c52-bb5e-8f3499aa046e\"]},{\"text\":\"Bidirectional sleep-mental health relationship: Sleep and mental health influence each other; anxiety and depression disturb sleep, while insufficient sleep wo","rsens emotional regulation, stress tolerance, and positive emotion.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"6b86335a-0ad4-72b2-bb3f-d7433f3a0ea3\"]},{\"text\":\"Recovery debt in training: When training stress repeatedly exceeds recovery capacity, individuals progress from functional fatigue to non-functional overreaching and potentially overtraining syndrome.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"f823c9f8-9fd0-7db3-931a-574c5002b2b1\"]},{\"text\":\"Stress → recovery → adaptation cycle: The body operates on a stress-recovery-adaptation cycle; chronic underrecovery (not occasional poor sleep) drives long-term physiological strain and disease risk.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\"]},{\"text\":\"Active recovery vs. complete inactivity: Active recovery (walking, moderate exercise, hobbies, socializing) can be more restorative than complete inactivity, depending on the type of fatigue being addressed.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"]}],\"prerequisiteKnowledge\":[{\"text\":\"Basic understanding of autonomic nervous system function (sympathetic vs. parasympathetic activation)\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"]},{\"text\":\"Familiarity with metabolic terminology (insulin sensitivity, metabolic syndrome)\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"]},{\"text\":\"Knowledge of inflammatory markers and immune signalling\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"cda37927-8591-7c52-bb5e-8f3499aa046e\"]},{\"text\":\"Understanding of exercise physiology concepts (training stress, adaptation)\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"f823c9f8-9fd0-7db3-931a-574c5002b2b1\"]},{\"text\":\"Awareness of cognitive domains (attention, working memory, executive function)\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\"]}],\"vocabulary\":[{\"term\":\"Memory consolidation\",\"definition\":\"The process by which the brain stabilizes and integrates newly acquired information during sleep\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"]},{\"term\":\"Sympathetic activation\",\"definition\":\"Increased activity of the ","sympathetic nervous system, associated with stress responses and cardiovascular strain\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"]},{\"term\":\"Insulin sensitivity\",\"definition\":\"The degree to which cells respond to insulin; reduced sensitivity impairs blood-sugar regulation\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"]},{\"term\":\"IL-6 (Interleukin-6)\",\"definition\":\"An inflammatory marker that increases after partial sleep deprivation\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"cda37927-8591-7c52-bb5e-8f3499aa046e\"]},{\"term\":\"C-reactive protein\",\"definition\":\"An inflammatory marker elevated following sleep deprivation, indicating increased inflammatory activity\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"cda37927-8591-7c52-bb5e-8f3499aa046e\"]},{\"term\":\"Non-functional overreaching\",\"definition\":\"A state where training stress exceeds recovery capacity, leading to persistent fatigue and declining performance\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"f823c9f8-9fd0-7db3-931a-574c5002b2b1\"]},{\"term\":\"Overtraining syndrome\",\"definition\":\"Severe condition resulting from chronic training-recovery imbalance, characterized by persistent fatigue, performance decline, and mood disturbance\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"f823c9f8-9fd0-7db3-931a-574c5002b2b1\"]},{\"term\":\"Chronic underrecovery\",\"definition\":\"Consistently inadequate recovery over extended periods, including insufficient sleep, lack of breaks, or prolonged psychological stress\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\"]},{\"term\":\"Active recovery\",\"definition\":\"Low-intensity activities (walking, stretching, hobbies) that support physiological and psychological restoration\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"]}],\"misconceptions\":[{\"misconception\":\"One poor night of sleep causes heart disease\",\"correction\":\"Cardiovascular risk accrues over months and years of chronic short sleep, not from isolated poor nights. The concern is repeated inadequate recovery.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"]},{\"misconception\":\"Sleeping fi","ve hours instead of eight gains three productive hours\",\"correction\":\"While clock hours increase, the quality of waking hours declines. Attention, creativity, memory, judgement, and reaction time deteriorate, so fewer well-rested hours can outperform longer exhausted periods.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"e312964f-0a23-7c7e-8697-7c3ad685eebe\"]},{\"misconception\":\"Rest means complete inactivity or lying in bed\",\"correction\":\"Active recovery (walking, moderate exercise, hobbies) can be more beneficial than complete inactivity. Someone mentally exhausted may benefit more from a walk than from lying in bed scrolling social media.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"]},{\"misconception\":\"More sleep is always healthier\",\"correction\":\"Excessive time in bed is not necessarily healthier. Very long habitual sleep is associated with poorer health outcomes, though long sleep may be a marker of underlying illness rather than the cause.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"]},{\"misconception\":\"Occasional poor sleep or a busy week causes major permanent harm\",\"correction\":\"An occasional poor night or busy week is unlikely to cause major permanent harm in healthy individuals. The clinical concern is chronic underrecovery sustained over months.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\"]}],\"assessmentQuestions\":[{\"question\":\"Describe the five active physiological processes that occur during adequate sleep and recovery, and explain why rest should not be considered 'doing nothing.'\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"]},{\"question\":\"A patient reports sleeping 5 hours nightly to 'gain productive time.' Using evidence from the source, explain why this strategy may be counterproductive for knowledge work.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\",\"e312964f-0a23-7c7e-8697-7c3ad685eebe\"]},{\"question\":\"Explain the mechanism linking chronic short sleep to cardiovascular disease, and clarify why a single poor ni","ght does not cause heart disease.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"]},{\"question\":\"Trace the metabolic pathway from sleep restriction to increased type 2 diabetes risk, including changes in insulin sensitivity and energy intake.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"]},{\"question\":\"Compare the immune consequences of partial sleep deprivation with the immune benefits of normal sleep, citing specific inflammatory markers.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"cda37927-8591-7c52-bb5e-8f3499aa046e\"]},{\"question\":\"An athlete presents with persistent fatigue, declining performance, and mood disturbance after months of intense training. Using the stress → recovery → adaptation model, explain the likely diagnosis and the importance of recovery.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"f823c9f8-9fd0-7db3-931a-574c5002b2b1\",\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\"]},{\"question\":\"A client asks whether they should rest completely in bed or take a walk after a mentally exhausting workday. Provide an evidence-based recommendation.\",\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"]}],\"focusCoverage\":{\"status\":\"covered\"}}"],"textChunksTruncated":false,"textCharacters":11133,"reasoningCharacters":0,"finishReason":"stop","inputTokens":"[REDACTED]","outputTokens":"[REDACTED]"},"retries":0,"latencyMs":35380,"service":"pipeline-worker","correlationId":"01a11850-8e35-73cc-a9b5-000a01137296"}}
