@avlp/pipeline-worker:dev: {"timestamp":"2026-10-07T21:44:19.958Z","level":"info","event":"provider.together.response","fields":{"provider":"together","endpoint":"/chat/completions","response":{"httpStatus":200,"contentType":"text/event-stream","providerRequestId":"1f4fb3e6-aws_ew1","model":"moonshotai/Kimi-K3","text":"{\"schemaVersion\":\"outline-v1\",\"targetDurationSeconds\":300,\"items\":[{\"kind\":\"hook\",\"title\":\"The Most Underrated Intervention in Medicine\",\"description\":\"Open by reframing rest for a clinical audience: not the absence of work but an active, multi-system physiological process with measurable downstream consequences when withheld. Frame the lesson's thrust — that the medical stakes of rest span neurocognitive function, cardiometabolic trajectories, and psychiatric comorbidity, and that chronic underrecovery, not single bad nights, is where pathology accrues.\",\"objectiveIds\":[\"01a11851-61ff-794c-ad56-b51525d9c4b2\"],\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"],\"framingNote\":\"Generated framing device that dramatizes the lesson focus; grounded in the source's claim that rest is part of the body's recovery system rather than 'doing nothing.'\",\"estimatedSeconds\":30},{\"kind\":\"concept\",\"title\":\"Rest as Active Physiology: Five Recovery Mechanisms\",\"description\":\"Teach the five mechanisms that execute during adequate sleep and recovery: memory consolidation, cardiovascular and metabolic recalibration, immune regulation, tissue repair after physical stress, and nervous-system downshift away from prolonged stress activation. Emphasize the evidence hierarchy — strongest for sleep, with additional support for work breaks and post-exercise recovery — and position rest mechanistically as an anabolic, regulatory window rather than passive downtime.\",\"objectiveIds\":[\"01a11851-61ff-794c-ad56-b51525d9c4b2\"],\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"],\"estimatedSeconds\":50},{\"kind\":\"concept\",\"title\":\"Neurocognitive and Affective Costs of Sleep Loss\",\"description\":\"Enumerate the specific deficits produced by sleep loss: impaired attention, slowed reaction time, degraded working memory, impaired learning and decision-making, and compromised emotional control, presenting clinically as poor concentration, forgetfulness, irritability, impaired judgement, low motivation","textChunks":["{\"schemaVersion\":\"outline-v1\",\"targetDurationSeconds\":300,\"items\":[{\"kind\":\"hook\",\"title\":\"The Most Underrated Intervention in Medicine\",\"description\":\"Open by reframing rest for a clinical audience: not the absence of work but an active, multi-system physiological process with measurable downstream consequences when withheld. Frame the lesson's thrust — that the medical stakes of rest span neurocognitive function, cardiometabolic trajectories, and psychiatric comorbidity, and that chronic underrecovery, not single bad nights, is where pathology accrues.\",\"objectiveIds\":[\"01a11851-61ff-794c-ad56-b51525d9c4b2\"],\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"],\"framingNote\":\"Generated framing device that dramatizes the lesson focus; grounded in the source's claim that rest is part of the body's recovery system rather than 'doing nothing.'\",\"estimatedSeconds\":30},{\"kind\":\"concept\",\"title\":\"Rest as Active Physiology: Five Recovery Mechanisms\",\"description\":\"Teach the five mechanisms that execute during adequate sleep and recovery: memory consolidation, cardiovascular and metabolic recalibration, immune regulation, tissue repair after physical stress, and nervous-system downshift away from prolonged stress activation. Emphasize the evidence hierarchy — strongest for sleep, with additional support for work breaks and post-exercise recovery — and position rest mechanistically as an anabolic, regulatory window rather than passive downtime.\",\"objectiveIds\":[\"01a11851-61ff-794c-ad56-b51525d9c4b2\"],\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\"],\"estimatedSeconds\":50},{\"kind\":\"concept\",\"title\":\"Neurocognitive and Affective Costs of Sleep Loss\",\"description\":\"Enumerate the specific deficits produced by sleep loss: impaired attention, slowed reaction time, degraded working memory, impaired learning and decision-making, and compromised emotional control, presenting clinically as poor concentration, forgetfulness, irritability, impaired judgement, low motivation",", and cognitive 'fog.' Weigh the evidence base: experimental findings aggregated across more than 150 studies showing consistent reduction of positive mood and elevation of anxiety symptoms.\",\"objectiveIds\":[\"01a11851-61ff-74f5-b924-85b091ea6b99\"],\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\"],\"estimatedSeconds\":45},{\"kind\":\"concept\",\"title\":\"Cardiovascular Strain: A Cumulative Temporal Trajectory\",\"description\":\"Trace the mechanistic chain from chronic short sleep to cardiovascular disease: sympathetic nervous system activation, inflammatory upregulation, blood-pressure dysregulation, and hormonal stress responses converging on hypertension, coronary heart disease, and stroke. Stress the critical clinical distinction — one poor night does not cause heart disease; risk accrues through repeated inadequate recovery over months and years, which has direct implications for patient counseling and risk framing.\",\"objectiveIds\":[\"01a11851-61ff-7838-9736-e9866515059d\"],\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\"],\"estimatedSeconds\":45},{\"kind\":\"concept\",\"title\":\"Metabolic Progression: From Sleep Restriction to Type 2 Diabetes\",\"description\":\"Connect the metabolic sequence stepwise: sleep restriction reduces insulin sensitivity; randomized trials demonstrate increased hunger and elevated caloric intake under sleep deprivation; over time this drives weight gain, insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome, and elevated type 2 diabetes risk. Highlight that the hunger and intake findings carry randomized-trial evidence, distinguishing them from purely observational associations.\",\"objectiveIds\":[\"01a11851-61ff-7976-b653-03c706cc708b\"],\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\"],\"estimatedSeconds\":45},{\"kind\":\"concept\",\"title\":\"The Bidirectional Sleep–Mental Health Loop\",\"description\":\"Analyze the reciprocal causality between sleep and psychiatric state: anxiety and depression disturb sleep, while insufficient or disrupted sleep degrades emotiona","l regulation. Detail the documented consequences of sleep deficiency — increased anxiety, depressive symptoms, greater emotional reactivity, poorer stress tolerance, and reduced positive emotion — and discuss the clinical implication that sleep disruption is simultaneously a symptom, a perpetuating factor, and a therapeutic entry point.\",\"objectiveIds\":[\"01a11851-61ff-74bb-82b8-cdcb8d5dc062\"],\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"6b86335a-0ad4-72b2-bb3f-d7433f3a0ea3\"],\"estimatedSeconds\":45},{\"kind\":\"example\",\"title\":\"Stress → Recovery → Adaptation, and Why Active Recovery Wins\",\"description\":\"Apply the stress → recovery → adaptation cycle to distinguish acute fatigue (an occasional poor night or busy week, unlikely to cause permanent harm in a healthy person) from chronic underrecovery (persistently short sleep, work without breaks, intense training without recovery, months of psychological stress), which the evidence links to poorer cognition, emotional problems, metabolic impairment, inflammation, weakened immune regulation, cardiovascular disease, injury, and reduced performance. Illustrate with the active-recovery contrast: the cognitively exhausted desk worker benefits more from a walk than from lying in bed scrolling social media, and note the nuance that very long habitual sleep associates with poorer outcomes, likely as a marker of underlying illness rather than a cause.\",\"objectiveIds\":[\"01a11851-61ff-7134-bcc9-d9ae12d758ca\"],\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\",\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\"],\"estimatedSeconds\":60},{\"kind\":\"summary\",\"title\":\"Clinical Synthesis: Rest as Prescribable Physiology\",\"description\":\"Consolidate the lesson: rest executes five active mechanisms; sleep loss produces quantifiable neurocognitive and affective deficits supported by 150+ studies; cardiovascular and metabolic harms follow cumulative, months-to-years trajectories; sleep and mental health degrade each other bidirectionally; and the stress → recovery → adaptation mo","del defines chronic underrecovery — not isolated fatigue — as the true clinical target, with active recovery often outperforming inactivity.\",\"objectiveIds\":[\"01a11851-61ff-794c-ad56-b51525d9c4b2\",\"01a11851-61ff-74f5-b924-85b091ea6b99\",\"01a11851-61ff-7838-9736-e9866515059d\",\"01a11851-61ff-7976-b653-03c706cc708b\",\"01a11851-61ff-74bb-82b8-cdcb8d5dc062\",\"01a11851-61ff-7134-bcc9-d9ae12d758ca\"],\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\",\"9eec6c71-abc2-7bc6-91ca-cd15dc252fb4\",\"0a0f0502-0564-77b7-8c06-23269be050cd\",\"a1a47b52-c81d-796a-b306-839490391e8d\",\"6b86335a-0ad4-72b2-bb3f-d7433f3a0ea3\",\"b874bce9-1d1f-70cb-8b51-1c9d1e7f9dd0\",\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\"],\"estimatedSeconds\":30}]}"],"textChunksTruncated":false,"textCharacters":6709,"reasoningCharacters":0,"finishReason":"stop","inputTokens":"[REDACTED]","outputTokens":"[REDACTED]"},"retries":0,"latencyMs":21332,"service":"pipeline-worker","correlationId":"01a11850-8e35-73cc-a9b5-000a01137296"}}
