@avlp/pipeline-worker:dev: {"timestamp":"2026-10-07T22:01:23.489Z","level":"info","event":"provider.together.response","fields":{"provider":"together","endpoint":"/chat/completions","response":{"httpStatus":200,"contentType":"text/event-stream","providerRequestId":"bfaabf54-aws_ew1","model":"moonshotai/Kimi-K3","text":"{\"schemaVersion\":\"scene-regeneration-v1\",\"mode\":\"regenerate\",\"scene\":{\"id\":\"01a1185b-0553-785f-a11d-5f9d47809673\",\"order\":1,\"template\":\"hook\",\"title\":\"The Most Underrated Intervention\",\"narration\":\"Rest is the most underrated intervention in clinical medicine. We treat it as downtime, as the absence of work, but physiologically it is anything but passive. Rest is an active component of the body's recovery apparatus, engaging multiple organ systems at once. Withhold it, and the consequences are measurable across neurocognitive performance, cardiometabolic trajectory, psychiatric comorbidity, and even immune regulation — controlled studies of partial sleep deprivation show impaired immune signalling alongside elevated inflammatory markers such as IL-6 and C-reactive protein. And the pathology does not accrue from a single bad night. It accrues from chronic underrecovery, sustained over months and years.\",\"onScreenText\":[],\"durationSeconds\":34,\"transition\":\"cut\",\"visual\":{\"question\":\"What if rest is an active prescription, not downtime?\",\"prompt\":\"Physiological stakes of recovery\",\"supportingElements\":[\"Cognitive\",\"Cardiometabolic\",\"Immune & inflammatory\"]},\"narrationBlockIds\":[\"01a11853-2f5a-7c2c-a213-7f4bbb8de8d0\"],\"estimatedSeconds\":36,\"sourceBlockIds\":[\"b168fb27-3f9e-791a-b576-c1319c55e426\",\"0dd73264-3a04-721d-b42a-7a779096a67c\"],\"generatedAdditions\":[],\"assetRequirements\":[{\"slot\":\"subject\",\"purpose\":\"Conceptual hero visual of a resting figure with subtle multi-system (brain, heart, immune) highlighting to frame rest as active physiology\"}]}}","textChunks":["{\"schemaVersi